NAME
    osarch - query, inspect and select active OS/architecture command roots

SYNOPSIS
    osarch
    osarch show
    osarch update
    osarch set <osarch>

DESCRIPTION
    osarch owns the active platform selection represented by:
        bin/sys-osarch
        bin/ext-osarch
        bin/ai-osarch

    All three selectors must be relative symbolic links to existing platform
    directories and must represent the same supported normalized osarch.

    With no arguments, osarch writes only the currently selected osarch followed
    by a newline.

    osarch show writes the selected osarch, its normalized operating-system and
    architecture components, and one block for each selector containing:
        link
            selector pathname
        target
            relative symbolic-link target
        resolved
            physical pathname reached by resolving the selector

    osarch update detects the host operating system and architecture through the
    m OS/architecture facility and selects the resulting normalized osarch.

    osarch set <osarch> selects the explicit supported normalized identity.

    The normalized identity has the form:
        <platform>-<architecture>

    Current platforms are linux, macos and windows. Current architectures are
    x86_64 and arm64.

    update and set ensure these directories exist:
        bin/sys-<osarch>
        bin/ext-<osarch>
        bin/ai-<osarch>

    and create or update these relative selector symlinks:
        bin/sys-osarch -> sys-<osarch>
        bin/ext-osarch -> ext-<osarch>
        bin/ai-osarch  -> ai-<osarch>

    Existing non-symlink objects at selector paths are not overwritten.


OUTPUT
    Bare osarch writes:
        <osarch>

    osarch show uses this form:
        osarch: <osarch>
        os: <platform>
        arch: <architecture>
        sys-osarch:
          link: <pathname>
          target: sys-<osarch>
          resolved: <physical-pathname>
        ext-osarch:
          link: <pathname>
          target: ext-<osarch>
          resolved: <physical-pathname>
        ai-osarch:
          link: <pathname>
          target: ai-<osarch>
          resolved: <physical-pathname>

    Successful update and set operations produce no output.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Query, display or selection completed successfully.
    Non-zero status indicates invalid arguments, an invalid/misaligned selector,
        unsupported detected platform data or filesystem/update failure.
